Encapsulator Design & Fabrication

The Agriguard control electronics are housed inside a custom-designed 3D-printed enclosure. The enclosure is designed using CAD software and fabricated using FDM (Fused Deposition Modelling) 3D printing. This allows the dimensions, mounting points, cable openings and component locations to be customised according to the electronic components used in the system.

The enclosure consists of a main body and removable cover. The main body provides the structural support for the ESP32 controller, relay/MOSFET circuitry, power connections and other control electronics, while the cover protects the components from accidental contact, dust and environmental exposure.

Component Fastening

Components are mounted using integrated mounting bosses/standoffs that are incorporated into the 3D-printed enclosure.

The ESP32 development board is mounted onto raised standoffs using small machine screws.
The relay/MOSFET control circuitry is mounted using separate screw holes or standoffs.
The LCD/display, where applicable, is secured into a dedicated opening in the enclosure.
Larger components are positioned so that they do not interfere with one another or with the enclosure cover.
Screws allow components to be removed for maintenance or replacement without destroying the enclosure.

Where appropriate, heat-set threaded inserts can be installed into the printed plastic. This provides stronger and more durable threads than repeatedly screwing directly into the printed material.

Electrical Connections

The electronics are connected using insulated wires and removable connectors, rather than permanently soldering every component directly to the enclosure.

Cable-entry points are incorporated into the enclosure for connections to:

Soil-moisture sensors
Temperature/humidity sensor
Water-flow sensor
Water-level sensor
Irrigation valves/pump
Power supply
Emergency-stop circuit

The cable openings can be fitted with rubber grommets or cable glands to reduce cable movement and provide additional protection against dust and moisture.

Enclosure Assembly

The general assembly process is:

CAD design → 3D print → install threaded inserts → mount electronics → connect wiring → close enclosure → test

The enclosure is designed so that the electronics can be assembled, inspected and serviced without having to redesign or reprint the entire housing.
